<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdullah Khan II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1533">1533</a>–<a href="/years/1598">1598</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdullah Khan II was the most powerful ruler of the Shaybanid dynasty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, effectively in power from the 1550s and khan from 1583 to 1598. He reunited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, bridges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>. Soon after his death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shaybanids</a> died out, and the Janids took the throne in 1599.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdullah-khan-ii.jpg" alt="Abdullah Khan II" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bukhara School · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdullah_Khan_Uzbek_II_slicing_melons.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abdullah Khan was born in 1533 or 1534 at Afarinkent, on an island between two arms of the Zarafshan River. After the death of Abdulaziz Shaybanid there was a struggle for the throne. After Abdullatif of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> died in 1551, Barak Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> took Samarkand and was declared supreme khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> as Newroz Ahmed Khan. Abdullah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, then governor of Kermine, led the resistance with his uncle Pir Muhammad Khan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, who was proclaimed supreme ruler after Newroz Ahmad died in 1556.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1557 Abdullah Sultan captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> sheikhs. In 1561 he proclaimed his father Iskander supreme khan, though his father, more interested in religious rites, left the conduct of state to him. He set out to combine Bukhara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tashkent">Tashkent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> in one state, which took many years: a long war for Tashkent, the conquest of Fergana, the taking of Balkh in 1573 after a long siege and of Tashkent in 1576, and a single state only in 1582. After his father's death in 1583 he was declared ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> and the state was called the Bukhara Khanate.</p><p>He seized Badakhshan in 1584 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> in 1588, and in 1593–94 his troops conquered Khorezm. A war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> lasted from 1587 to 1598, and a non-aggression pact with the Mughal Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>, recognising Akbar's rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, left him free to fight it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His monetary reforms, including a 1583 reform centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins for daily trade, ended a monetary crisis and stopped currency leaking out of the state. He encouraged trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> and bridges, including the Kosh-Madrasah in Bukhara. His court included the poet Abd al-Rahman Mushfiqi and the historian Hafiz Tanish, and he wrote poems under the name Avaz Gazi.</p><p>He died in 1598 and was buried at the mausoleum of Bahauddin Naqshbandi near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>. His only son Abd al-Mumin was soon killed by rebels, and Baqi Muhammad, a son of Abdullah's sister, defeated and killed Pir Muhammad II in battle. With this the Shaybanid dynasty ended and Baqi Muhammad became the first khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janid-dynasty">Janid dynasty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdullah Khan II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Abdullah Khan II</b> reunited the realm in the late 16th century, building caravanserais, bridges and madrasas.</blockquote><a href="/state/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Khanate of Bukhara. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.66,66.98&amp;year=1575" aria-label="Samarkand on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Abdullah Khan II"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5458 4164h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5458 4164h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.66,66.98&amp;year=1575">What was here in 1575? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
